> Ô_i ~ Poisson(mu_i) where mu_i=exp(log(E_i) + a + b*X_i + S_i +H_i)
> Ô’_i ~ Poisson(mu’_i) where mu’_i=exp(log(E_i) + a + b*z_i + S_i
> +H_i)
>
> And finally obtain the excess of death by summing the excess deaths
> (Ô_i – Ô’_i) across all areas.
>
> With INLA I can predict and obtain the posterior distributions of Ô_i
> and Ô’_i. But I don’t know if it would be possible obtain de posterior
> distribution of Ô_i – Ô’_i for each area. And finally the posterior
> distribution of the sum of Ô_i – Ô’_I across all the areas. Moreover,
> I’m interested in obtaining the credible intervals of this excess
> number of deaths.

what you get is to get the marginal for log(O_i) - log(O_j), and also
the posterior correlation matrix better log(O_i) and log(O_j).

having the marginal for log(O_i) and log(O_j) and the correlation
between log(O_i) and log(O_j), cries out for a Gaussian copula with
these marginals and correlation, then you have the joint (approx) for
O_i and O_j; see

http://www.r-inla.org/faq#TOC-I-want-to-compute-approximate-the-j

